<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Ouchi clan domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1557 CE (approx.) · capital Yamaguchi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The realm of the Ouchi lords of western Honshu, who grew rich on trade with Ming China and Korea and made Yamaguchi a little Kyoto.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-clan">Ouchi</a>, who claimed descent from a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/baekje">Baekje</a> prince, were long established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suo-province">Suo</a> province. In the late 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-yoshihiro">Ouchi Yoshihiro</a> held six provinces and controlled trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, but he rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a> and died in 1399; his successors rebuilt the family's power across Suo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagato-province">Nagato</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> from about 1400.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>As military governors the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-clan">Ouchi</a> built a strong domain with their own house law. They dominated the official tally trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming China</a> after 1523 and traded with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/joseon">Joseon</a>, using the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakata">Hakata</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamaguchi-city">Yamaguchi</a> became a cultural centre where the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesshu-toyo">Sesshu</a> worked, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-yoshitaka">Ouchi Yoshitaka</a> received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> in 1551.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1551 the retainer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sue-harukata">Sue Harukata</a> overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-yoshitaka">Yoshitaka</a>, who killed himself, and set up a puppet lord. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-motonari">Mori Motonari</a> defeated Sue at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-miyajima">Itsukushima</a> in 1555 and destroyed the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-clan">Ouchi</a> in 1557, taking over their lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ouchi-clan-domain.jpg" alt="Ouchi clan domain" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Mukai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Crest_Oouchi_Hisi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1557 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yamaguchi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ouchi Yoshihiro, Ouchi Masahiro, Ouchi Yoshitaka</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ouchi clan, Ouchi of Suo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1478&amp;focus=ouchi-clan-domain" aria-label="Ouchi clan domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8027 939 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ouchi clan domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8347 1195L8441 1168"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8441 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8347 1195h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8347 1195h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yamaguchi. <a href="/atlas?year=1478&amp;focus=ouchi-clan-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
